@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')
  Views
@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>The panel's screens are Propel views in the <code>ignite</code> namespace. The app can add its own pages in <code>resources/views/</code>, and they can extend the panel's layout.</p>

  <h2>The ignite namespace</h2>
  <p><code>Ignite::new()</code> registers the <code>ignite</code> namespace. The views in it are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>ignite::layouts.app</code>, the page frame, with the header and navigation.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::login</code>, the sign-in page.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::dashboard</code>, the stat cards.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::index</code>, the list of a resource.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::form</code>, the create and edit pages.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::partials.flash</code>, the message shown after a change.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Extend the layout</h2>
  <p>A view of the app extends the panel's layout and fills its <code>title</code> and <code>content</code> sections. It needs only its own variables. This view shows the signed-in user, and the route in <a href="/docs/panel">Panel</a> renders it with <code>user_id</code>:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@extends('ignite::layouts.app')

@@section('title')
  Account
@@endsection

@@section('content')
  &lt;h1&gt;Account&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Signed in as user @{{ $user_id }}.&lt;/p&gt;
@@endsection</code></pre>
  <p>The view is <code>resources/views/account.propel</code>, and the view name is <code>account</code>.</p>
  <p>The layout also yields three sections for the panel's own chrome. The panel's pages set them, and an app view can set them too:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>ignite_home</code>, the brand link in the header. It defaults to <code>/admin</code>.</li>
    <li><code>ignite_nav</code>, the navigation and the sign-out button. It is empty by default, so an app view has no navigation unless it sets this section.</li>
    <li><code>ignite_flash</code>, the message shown after a change. It is empty by default.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The panel does not let an app replace one of its own views. In v1, only the sections above can change.</p>

  <h2>Where the views are read from</h2>
  <p>Propel reads view files from disk, so the panel's views must exist on disk when the app runs. Their folder is chosen in this order:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>Ignite::views_dir</code>, in the builder, which wins over the environment.</li>
    <li>The <code>IGNITE_VIEWS_DIR</code> environment variable.</li>
    <li>The <code>views/ignite</code> folder of the Ignite source, which is the default. That folder exists on the machine that built the crate, not on a deployed server.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The folder must contain <code>layouts/app.propel</code>. <code>fairing()</code> panics with the fix in its message when no folder is set or the views are missing.</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_panel::Ignite;

/// The panel with its views in a directory of the deployed app.
pub fn panel() -&gt; Ignite {
    Ignite::new().views_dir("/srv/ignite/views")
}</code></pre>
  <p>The views are not embedded in the binary. Propel has no hook for another loader, so a deployed app ships the <code>views/ignite</code> folder and sets <code>IGNITE_VIEWS_DIR</code>.</p>
@endsection
